{"id":"workloads-pods-advanced-pod-config","corpus":"k8s","title":"Advanced Pod Configuration","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/advanced-pod-config/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Advanced Pod Configuration"},{"kind":"p","text":"This page covers advanced Pod configuration topics including PriorityClasses, RuntimeClasses, security context within Pods, and introduces aspects of scheduling."},{"kind":"h2","text":"PriorityClasses"},{"kind":"p","text":"PriorityClasses allow you to set the importance of Pods relative to other Pods. If you assign a priority class to a Pod, Kubernetes sets the .spec.priority field for that Pod based on the PriorityClass you specified (you cannot set .spec.priority directly). If or when a Pod cannot be scheduled, and the problem is due to a lack of resources, the kube-scheduler tries to preempt lower priority Pods, in order to make scheduling of the higher priority Pod possible."},{"kind":"p","text":"A PriorityClass is a cluster-scoped API object that maps a priority class name to an integer priority value. Higher numbers indicate higher priority."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Built-in PriorityClasses"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes provides two built-in PriorityClasses:"},{"kind":"li","text":"system-cluster-critical: For system components that are critical to the cluster"},{"kind":"li","text":"system-node-critical: For system components that are critical to individual nodes. This is the highest priority that Pods can have in Kubernetes."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information, see Pod Priority and Preemption."},{"kind":"h2","text":"RuntimeClasses"},{"kind":"p","text":"A RuntimeClass allows you to specify the low-level container runtime for a Pod. It is useful when you want to specify different container runtimes for different kinds of Pod, such as when you need different isolation levels or runtime features."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Example Pod"},{"kind":"p","text":"A RuntimeClass is a cluster-scoped object that represents a container runtime that is available on some or all of your node."},{"kind":"p","text":"The cluster administrator installs and configures the concrete runtimes backing the RuntimeClass."},{"kind":"p","text":"They might set up that special container runtime configuration on all nodes, or perhaps just on some of them."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information, see the RuntimeClass documentation."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Pod and container level security context configuration"},{"kind":"p","text":"The Security context field in the Pod specification provides granular control over security settings for Pods and containers."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Pod-wide securityContext"},{"kind":"p","text":"Some aspects of security apply to the whole Pod; for other aspects, you might want to set a default, without any container-level overrides."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here's an example of using securityContext at the Pod level:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Container-level security context"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can specify the security context just for a specific container. Here's an example:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Security context options"},{"kind":"li","text":"User and Group IDs: Control which user/group the container runs as"},{"kind":"li","text":"Capabilities: Add or drop Linux capabilities"},{"kind":"li","text":"Seccomp Profiles: Set security computing profiles"},{"kind":"li","text":"SELinux Options: Configure SELinux context"},{"kind":"li","text":"AppArmor: Configure AppArmor profiles for additional access control"},{"kind":"li","text":"Windows Options: Configure Windows-specific security settings"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can also use the Pod securityContext to allow privileged mode in Linux containers. Privileged mode overrides many of the other security settings in the securityContext. Avoid using this setting unless you can't grant the equivalent permissions by using other fields in the securityContext. You can run Windows containers in a similarly privileged mode by setting the windowsOptions.hostProcess flag on the Pod-level security context. For details and instructions, see Create a Windows HostProcess Pod."},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information, see Configure a Security Context for a Pod or Container."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Influencing Pod scheduling decisions"},{"kind":"p","text":"Kubernetes provides several mechanisms to control which nodes your Pods are scheduled on."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Node selectors"},{"kind":"p","text":"The simplest form of node selection constraint:"},{"kind":"h3","text":"Node affinity"},{"kind":"p","text":"Node affinity allows you to specify rules that constrain which nodes your Pod can be scheduled on. Here's an example of a Pod that prefers running on nodes labelled as being on a particular continent, selecting based on the value of topology.kubernetes.io/zone label."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Pod affinity and anti-affinity"},{"kind":"p","text":"In addition to node affinity, you can also constrain which nodes a Pod can be scheduled on based on the labels of other Pods that are already running on nodes. Pod affinity allows you to specify rules about where a Pod should be placed relative to other Pods."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Tolerations"},{"kind":"p","text":"Tolerations allow Pods to be scheduled on nodes with matching taints:"},{"kind":"p","text":"For more information, see Assign Pods to Nodes."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Pod overhead"},{"kind":"p","text":"Pod overhead allows you to account for the resources consumed by the Pod infrastructure on top of the container requests and limits."},{"kind":"h2","text":"What's next"},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about Pod Priority and Preemption"},{"kind":"li","text":"Read about RuntimeClasses"},{"kind":"li","text":"Explore Configure a Security Context for a Pod or Container"}],"lemmas":{"advanced":["advanced","advance"],"advance